\nconst DAMAGE_HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 8;\nconst DAMAGE_HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE = 0.5;\n\ntype Damage = { itemType: string; amount: number };\n\ntype Incident = { cause: string; damages: Damage[] };\n\ntype PolicyState = { remainingCap?: number };\n\ntype ClaimResult = { payout: number; remainingCap: number };\n\nconst findInsuredItem = (policyItems: Item[], itemType: string): Item | undefined =>\n  policyItems.find((item) => item.type === itemType);\n\nconst computeDamagePayout = (damage: Damage, policyItems: Item[]): number => {\n  const item = findInsuredItem(policyItems, damage.itemType);\n  const reimbursable =\n    item && meetsEnchantmentThreshold(item, DAMAGE_HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD)\n      ? damage.amount * DAMAGE_HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE\n      : damage.amount;\n  return Math.max(0, reimbursable - DEDUCTIBLE);\n};\n\nconst sumDamagePayouts = (damages: Damage[], policyItems: Item[]): number =>\n  damages.reduce((sum, damage) => sum + computeDamagePayout(damage, policyItems), 0);\n\nexport const processClaim = (\n  policyItems: Item[],\n  incident: Incident,\n  policyState: PolicyState = {}\n): ClaimResult => {\n  const availableCap = policyState.remainingCap ?? computeCap(policyItems);\n  const desiredPayout = roundDownToWholeG(sumDamagePayouts(incident.damages, policyItems));\n  const payout = Math.min(desiredPayout, availableCap);\n\n[4 more lines in file. Use offset=144 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784914732425},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Refactoring Report\n\n**Naming Evaluation (First Priority)**:\n- `processClaim`: still clearly describes its purpose — processes an insurance claim against a policy and returns payout + remaining cap. The name fits the expanded behavior (successive claims via `policyState`) without change.\n- `availableCap`, `desiredPayout`, `payout`, `remainingCap`: all read as clear, self-documenting explaining variables. No renames needed.\n\nDecision: Keep all existing names — they already reveal intent accurately.\n\n**Current Code Mass** (processClaim function body, before refactor):\n```ts\nexport const processClaim = (\n  policyItems: Item[],\n  incident: { cause: string; damages: Damage[] },\n  policyState: { remainingCap?: number } = {}\n): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } => {\n  const availableCap = policyState.remainingCap ?? computeCap(policyItems);\n  const desiredPayout = roundDownToWholeG(sumDamagePayouts(incident.damages, policyItems));\n  const payout = Math.min(desiredPayout, availableCap);\n  const remainingCap = availableCap - payout;\n  return { payout, remainingCap };\n};\n```\n- Bindings: policyItems, incident, policyState, availableCap, desiredPayout, payout, remainingCap = 7\n- Invocations: computeCap, roundDownToWholeG, sumDamagePayouts, Math.min = 4 × 2 = 8\n- Conditionals: `??` (nullish coalescing) = 1 × 4 = 4\n- Constants: 0 literals used directly\n- Loops: 0\n- Assignments: 0 (all `const`)\n\nTotal Mass ≈ 7 + 8 + 4 = **19**\n\n**Applied Refactoring — Rule 4 (Fewest Elements) via extracted type aliases**\n\nThe inline object-literal types for `incident`, `policyState`, and the return type were repeated as anonymous shapes directly in the function signature — a form of structural duplication (Rule 3) that also obscured intent (Rule 2), since a reader had to parse three inline object types just to see the function's contract. I extracted them into named type aliases:\n\n```ts\ntype Incident = { cause: string; damages: Damage[] };\ntype PolicyState = { remainingCap?: number };\ntype ClaimResult = { payout: number; remainingCap: number };\n\nexport const processClaim = (\n  policyItems: Item[],\n  incident: Incident,\n  policyState: PolicyState = {}\n): ClaimResult => {\n  const availableCap = policyState.remainingCap ?? computeCap(policyItems);\n  const desiredPayout = roundDownToWholeG(sumDamagePayouts(incident.damages, policyItems));\n  const payout = Math.min(desiredPayout, availableCap);\n  const remainingCap